{{History of Balisca}}

The '''Siege of Castejón''' ({{lang-bl|Sitio de Castejon}}) was one of the first major engagements of the [[Baliscan Civil War]], in which the newly formed Bayaran Army attempted to capture the city of Castejón and the wider [[Alpujarra]].

The Bayaran secession and mobilization against the [[Southern Confederation|Confederal Convention]] led to it's early termination without an definitive agreement. Around the time of the outbreak of the [[Baliscan Civil War]], Castejón was considered to be the [[W:Frontier|frontier]] between the fledgling confederation and the [[Maroon Confederacy]], effectively a settlement in the "middle ground" in between cultures, peoples, and beyond the control of any authority. 

The Bayaran armies crossed the [[Cerroitá Pass]] and invaded the [[Alpujarra]] in the spring of 1828. The goal of this campaign was to gain control of a mountain pass through which they would be able to directly threaten the Hesperian mainland, and sever any land routes for a potential invasion of Bayara itself. The Bayarans easily swept south and besieged the settlement of Castejón. 

The siege was broken on 23 March 1829 following the intervention of the [[Maroon Confederacy]] on the side of the Confederalists, and the Bayarans ultimately withdrew. 

